 "rationale": "Single-process single-GPU offline batch=32 ResNet-18 forward; objective latency_ms is the wall time of one complete forward between CUDA syncs (independently measured original baseline 4.2388 ms). Measured evidence: in the execution_timeline diagnostic window the GPU is kernel-active 4068.5 of 4145.5 us (98.1%, 77.0 us inter-kernel gaps), so host dispatch/launch overhead is a minor lever (<=~170 us clean-vs-active). Leaf CUDA work per forward is a strictly sequential chain (leaf family sum 4141.9 us ~= 4142.0 us region total, no overlap): aten::conv2d 3040.7 us (73.4%), aten::batch_norm 426.0 us (10.3%), aten::relu 342.8 us (8.3%), aten::max_pool2d 198.9 us (4.8%), residual aten::add_ 120.4 us (2.9%). Conv kernels (cudnn scudnn/winograd heuristics) are frozen by the preserved numerics policy (TF32 off for matmul and cudnn, cudnn.benchmark=False, float32_matmul_precision='highest'), so the realistic opportunity is the 889 us of separate inference pointwise passes plus the stem pool: bn_fw_inf_1C11 (~450 GB/s effective) and clamp/relu (~470 GB/s) each read+write the full activation, and the stem conv->BN->ReLU->maxpool sequence does three full 25.7M-element passes (225.2+217.1+198.9 us) before a 4x downsample. Inference-mode BN is a per-channel affine transform that can be folded into the preceding conv's weights/bias or fused with ReLU and the residual add into single fp32 pointwise kernels, and the stem affine+ReLU+maxpool can be fused into one pass; these chains cross ResNetConvLayer, ResNetShortCut and MaxPool2d module boundaries, so the coherent editable boundary is the entire ResNetForImageClassification.forward region (one module boundary containing all 351 forward-phase calls) rather than many per-module patches. Unknowns: CUDA durations are diagnostic/inclusive (not clean wall time), GPU readiness/consumer completion is unobserved, and no candidate exists yet, so the benefit estimate below is a hypothesis the framework must test.",
 "hypothesis": "Replacing the whole forward region with one fp32 implementation that (a) folds each inference BatchNorm2d's affine parameters into the preceding conv's weight/bias or, equivalently, emits one fused [BN-affine + ReLU (+ residual add)] pointwise kernel per conv/shortcut site instead of separate bn_fw_inf and clamp passes, and (b) optionally absorbs the stem ReLU+max_pool2d into a single fused pass, while leaving the conv algorithm selection, precision policies, workload, and quality gates untouched, will remove an estimated 440-700 us of the 4142 us forward: most of the 426 us BN kernels plus roughly half of the 342.8 us ReLU and part of the 199 us stem maxpool and 77-170 us dispatch overhead. Expected clean latency_ms ~3.5-3.8 ms vs the 4.2388 ms baseline (~10-16% reduction), with logits within atol=rtol=1e-4 of the reference (BN folding/fusion only reorders fp32 rounding, ~1e-6 per layer) and identical top-1 labels. This is a testable hypothesis, not a guaranteed result: the diagnostic kernel times are from a perturbed capture, a faster local kernel need not improve the clean wall metric if a new bottleneck (e.g., remaining conv time) dominates, and acceptance requires the framework's independent clean measurement plus the source quality checks.",
